{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "Let n be any natural number, let (X_j) indexed by Fin(n+1) be measurable spaces, and let μ be a finite measure on their dependent product Ω = ∏_j X_j. Fix i ∈ Fin(n+1). Assume X_i is a nonempty standard Borel space and the complementary product Ω_{-i} = ∏_{j∈Fin(n)} X_{i.succAbove(j)} has measurable singletons. Write r_i(x) = (x_{i.succAbove(j)})_j and e_i(x) = (r_i(x), x_i). Let κ be the selected regular conditional-distribution kernel of the second coordinate given the first under (e_i)_#μ, and define H_i(x) = (e_i^{-1})_#(δ_{r_i(x)} × κ(r_i(x))). Then, for every x ∈ Ω whose complementary-coordinate fiber F_x = {y ∈ Ω : r_i(y) = r_i(x)} has nonzero μ-measure, H_i(x) = (μ(F_x))^{-1} • (μ restricted to F_x). Thus the coordinate heat-bath measure at every such x equals μ conditioned on that fiber.",
  "objects": "The family of measurable spaces X_j; the full dependent product Ω; the complementary-coordinate product Ω_{-i}; a finite measure μ on Ω; the selected coordinate i; a configuration x; the complementary-coordinate projection r_i; the measurable equivalence e_i; the selected conditional-distribution kernel κ; the coordinate heat-bath measure H_i(x); and the fiber F_x.",
  "domains": "n ranges over ℕ, including zero; the original coordinate index is Fin(n+1), and the complementary index is Fin(n), enumerated by i.succAbove. Each X_j is a type in an arbitrary universe equipped with a measurable space. μ is a measure on Ω = ∏_{j∈Fin(n+1)} X_j. i belongs to Fin(n+1), x belongs to Ω, κ maps Ω_{-i} to measures on X_i, and both sides of the asserted equality are measures on Ω.",
  "quantifiers": "For every natural number n, every family X of measurable spaces indexed by Fin(n+1), every finite measure μ on their dependent product, every coordinate i satisfying the stated coordinate and complementary-product assumptions, and every configuration x, if μ(F_x) ≠ 0, then the asserted equality of measures holds.",
  "assumptions": "Every X_j has a measurable-space structure; μ has finite total mass; the selected space X_i is standard Borel and nonempty; every singleton in the complementary dependent product is measurable; and the particular fiber F_x has nonzero μ-measure. No assumption that μ has total mass one is made, and no standard Borel assumption is imposed on the other coordinate spaces.",
  "conclusion": "CoordinateHeatBath.heatBath X μ i x equals ProbabilityTheory.cond μ F_x, namely (μ(F_x))^{-1} • μ.restrict F_x, as an equality of measures on the full dependent product. Under the hypotheses, the fiber mass is positive and finite, so this is normalized restriction to the fiber.",
  "scopes": "The equality is pointwise for every x satisfying the nonzero-fiber-mass hypothesis, not merely μ-almost everywhere. The conclusion is restricted to such fibers and makes no assertion for zero-mass fibers. The standard Borel and nonemptiness requirements concern only the selected coordinate i; the measurable-singleton requirement concerns the whole complementary product. The conditional-distribution kernel is the selected kernel specified in the definition context, whose general characterization is almost everywhere.",
  "constant_dependencies": "There are no numerical constants, unspecified bounds, or existential constants. H_i is determined by X, μ, i, and the selected conditional-distribution construction; F_x depends on i and r_i(x). The normalization factor is exactly the ENNReal inverse of μ(F_x).",
